<script module>
  import Demo from "$ui/components/Demo.abide"
  import Panel from "$ui/components/Panel.abide"
  import BranchScriptDemo from "$ui/demos/templating/scripts/BranchScriptDemo.abide"
  import MultilineScriptDemo from "$ui/demos/templating/scripts/MultilineScriptDemo.abide"
</script>

<main class="page">
  <h1>Branch-local <code>&lt;script&gt;</code></h1>
  <p class="lead">A <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> at the top of a block body is that <strong>branch's</strong> setup: its <code>state</code> is created when the branch mounts and disposed when it goes, and inside a <code>&#123;#for&#125;</code> that means <strong>per iteration</strong>. It reuses the component's imports and reads its bindings, but nothing outside the branch can see what it declares — including a sibling branch that happens to use the same name.</p>
  <Demo id="branch" title="Per-iteration <code>&lt;script&gt;</code>" client="src/ui/demos/templating/scripts/BranchScriptDemo.abide" tab="client">
    <BranchScriptDemo />
  </Demo>
  <aside class="aside">
    <p>Each row here owns a counter the component's own script has no name for. Adding a row adds a fresh one; the rows never share.</p>
  </aside>
  <Panel id="illegal-positions" title="Where a branch-local <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> may not go">
    <ul>
      <li>inside an element (<code>&lt;div&gt;&lt;script&gt;</code>) — the element has no lifetime of its own; move it to the enclosing block.</li>
      <li>after other markup in the body — it is setup, so it goes first.</li>
      <li>a second one in the same body, or a second root-level <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> — merge them.</li>
      <li><code>&lt;script module&gt;</code> — it runs once per module, so it belongs at the top level.</li>
    </ul>
  </Panel>
  <aside class="aside">
    <p>A branch-local <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> must be the <strong>first node of a block body</strong> — the body of an <code>&#123;#if&#125;</code> branch, a <code>&#123;#for&#125;</code>, an <code>&#123;#await&#125;</code> branch, a <code>&#123;:case&#125;</code>, a <code>&#123;#try&#125;</code> branch, or a <code>&#123;#component&#125;</code>. That is the only position with a lifetime and a scope of its own, so anywhere else is a compile error rather than a script that quietly never runs.</p>
    <p>It carries no <code>import</code> of its own — an import is module-level wherever it is written, so one here could only look conditional without being it. The component's imports are already in scope, which is why the demo above calls <code>state</code> without importing it a second time.</p>
  </aside>
  <h2>An assignment may span lines</h2>
  <p>Writing to a cell compiles to <code>cell.set(&hellip;)</code>, so the compiler has to find where the right-hand side actually ENDS. A line break next to a continuation operator is not the end — a ternary broken before its <code>?</code> and <code>:</code>, an operator with the break after it, a member chain broken before the <code>.</code>, an <code>instanceof</code>, an <code>in</code>, an <code>as</code> cast, or a template literal spanning lines are all one expression, exactly as they are in plain JavaScript.</p>
  <Demo id="multiline" title="A right-hand side that spans lines" client="src/ui/demos/templating/scripts/MultilineScriptDemo.abide" tab="client">
    <MultilineScriptDemo />
  </Demo>
  <aside class="aside">
    <p>This one is here because getting it wrong is <strong>silent</strong>. Severing the right-hand side at the break emits <code>size.set( count &gt; 2)</code> followed by an orphaned <code>? "many" : "few"</code> — which is not a syntax error, so nothing downstream complains. The cell is quietly set to the CONDITION and the branches evaluate against a discarded result. Every value on the page is still a value; it is just the wrong one, which is why the guard is a rendered demo rather than a compile check.</p>
  </aside>
</main>
